From: pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] blur-effect: Blur at a quarter of the resolution at least

ShellBlurEffect downscaled only once the radius passed 12 px, so a blur
that starts small, as the phone's notification shade's does (1 px and
up), began on full-size framebuffers and reallocated them at each step
of the factor as it grew: 1080x2280 buffers in the shade's first frames
on the S10e, and a 46 ms first frame.

SurfaceFlinger's Kawase blur works at a quarter of the resolution
whatever the radius (kInputScale). Do the same as soon as there is a
blur at all, as long as the downscaled size stays above
MIN_DOWNSCALE_SIZE.

For gnome-shell-mobile 51.1.mobile.22 (/usr/src/gnome-mobile/gnome-shell
on the S10e, built into /opt/gnome-mobile by build51.sh).
---
diff --git a/src/shell-blur-effect.c b/src/shell-blur-effect.c
index d362b0a..7382bde 100644
--- a/src/shell-blur-effect.c
+++ b/src/shell-blur-effect.c
@@ -52,6 +52,7 @@ static const gchar *brightness_glsl =
 
 #define MIN_DOWNSCALE_SIZE 256.f
 #define MAX_RADIUS 12.f
+#define MIN_DOWNSCALE_FACTOR 4.f
 
 typedef enum
 {
@@ -296,6 +297,23 @@ calculate_downscale_factor (float width,
   float scaled_height = height;
   float scaled_radius = radius;
 
+  /* A quarter of the resolution at least, once there is a blur at all, as
+   * SurfaceFlinger's Kawase blur has whatever the radius (kInputScale). A blur
+   * that starts small, as the phone's notification shade's does, otherwise
+   * began on full-size framebuffers and reallocated them at every step of
+   * the factor as it grew.
+   */
+  while (radius > 0 && downscale_factor < MIN_DOWNSCALE_FACTOR &&
+         width / (downscale_factor * 2.f) >= MIN_DOWNSCALE_SIZE &&
+         height / (downscale_factor * 2.f) >= MIN_DOWNSCALE_SIZE)
+    {
+      downscale_factor *= 2.f;
+
+      scaled_width = width / downscale_factor;
+      scaled_height = height / downscale_factor;
+      scaled_radius = radius / downscale_factor;
+    }
+
   /* This is the algorithm used by Firefox; keep downscaling until either the
    * blur radius is lower than the threshold, or the downscaled texture is too
    * small.
